{{!--
  Single-column landing, a bundle-added sibling of `home.hbs` — no Figma
  frame of its own; the design only draws the two-column layout that file
  renders. Selected the same way, with `:page-layout: home-single` on the
  page itself.

  Identical partial tree to `home.hbs` — same head, header, side menu, hero
  (home-hero.hbs, unchanged) and site footer — and it reuses that file's own
  `.home__hero` / `.home__main` markup verbatim rather than inventing new
  classes. The ONLY difference is the `home--single` modifier on `<main>`:
  home.css already stacks the hero above the content into one column below
  breakpoint `s` (nothing to stick a sticky aside beside once the columns
  become rows), so a single-column mode is that same stacked arrangement
  held at every breakpoint instead of only the narrow ones. See
  home-single.css for the (small) set of overrides that do it — it holds no
  typography or spacing of its own, only column/stickiness overrides scoped
  to `.home--single`.

  Because the hero and content column classes are unchanged, every authoring
  attribute `home-hero.hbs` documents (`:page-tags:`, `:page-action:`,
  `:page-hero-image:`, …) and every content-block convention `home.css`
  documents (the `.sect1`/`#preamble` heading rule, block rhythm) apply here
  exactly as they do on the two-column landing — a page picks the column
  count, not a different authoring surface.
--}}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en"{{#if page.home}} data-page-home{{/if}}>
  <head>
{{> head defaultPageTitle='Home'}}
  </head>
  <body class="home-page{{#with (or page.attributes.role page.role)}} {{{this}}}{{/with}}">
{{> header}}
{{!-- GH-197: page-level overlay — see home.hbs's own copy of this comment. --}}
{{> image-zoom-dialog}}
    <div class="body">
{{> nav}}
      <main class="home home--single dt-grid-layout dt-grid-layout--with-margin">
        <aside class="home__hero">{{> home-hero}}</aside>
        <div class="home__main doc">{{{page.contents}}}</div>
      </main>
    </div>
{{> site-footer}}
{{> footer-scripts}}
  </body>
</html>
